The == operator in Java compares the memory addresses of the objects, not their actual content. This means two objects with the same content will not necessarily be equal to each other, but two distinct objects with the same content will be. The .equals() method, on the other hand, compares the actual content of the objects.

The == operator is not always bad, but it can lead to bugs especially when comparing objects that are mutable or when the objects are intended to be distinct even though they contain the same value.

To compare strings in Java, you should use the .equals() method instead of the == operator because it compares the actual content of the strings, not their memory addresses. The .equals() method is the standard way to compare objects in Java and is the correct method to use when you want to ensure that the contents of the strings are identical.

Here's an example:

```java
String str1 = "Hello";
String str2 = "Hello";

// Using == to compare
if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("The strings are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are not equal");
} // Output: The strings are not equal

// Using .equals() to compare
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("The strings are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are not equal");
} // Output: The strings are equal
```

In this example, although both `str1` and `str2` contain the same text, `==` compares their memory addresses, which are different, while `.equals()` compares the actual contents, which are identical. Therefore, the `.equals()` method returns `true`, while `==` returns `false`.

